"[PDF p5]\n07 July 2026 ADP 6-0 iii\nPreface\nADP 6-0, Command and Control, provides doctrine on the fundamentals of command and control and the\ncommand and control warfighting function. Additionally, it describes the mission command approach to\ncommand and control. Through this approach, commanders combine the art and science of command and\ncontrol to understand situations, make decisions, direct actions, and lead forces toward mission\naccomplishment.\nApplicable to all members of the Army profession, the principal audience for ADP 6-0 is Army commanders,\nleaders, and unit staffs. It applies to Army forces during the conduct of operations and to Army forces\nperforming duties at a home station. ADP 6-0 provides the foundation for training and Army education\nsystem curricula concerning command and control of Army forces.\nTo comprehend ADP 6-0, readers should understand the fundamentals of multidomain operations described\nin ADP 3-0 and the fundamentals of leadership described in ADP 6-22. As the operations process is the\nframework for exercising command and control, readers must also understand the fundamentals of the\noperations process established in ADP 5-0. Details on the command and control system are found in FM 6-\n0.\nADP 6-0 implements North Atlantic Treaty Organization standardization agreement 2199. The Army\nhistorically fights with joint and multinational partners as part of a coalition, and ADP 6-0 is nested with joint\nand multinational doctrine. Army headquarters serving as a joint task force or multinational headquarters\nshould also refer to applicable joint or multinational doctrine concerning command and control of joint or\nmultinational forces.\nCommanders, staffs, and subordinates ensure their decisions and actions comply with applicable U.S.,\ninternational, and host nation laws and regulations when applying this doctrine. Commanders at all levels\nensure their Soldiers operate in accordance with the law of armed conflict and the rules of engagement as\ndiscussed in FM 6-27/ MCTP 11-10C. They also adhere to the Army ethic described in ADP 6-22.\nADP 6-0 uses joint terms where applicable. Selected joint and Army terms and definitions appear in both the\nglossary and the text. Terms for which ADP 6-0 is the proponent publication are marked with an asterisk (*)\nin the glossary. Definitions for which ADP 6-0 is the proponent publication are boldfaced in the text. For\nother definitions shown in the text, the term is italicized and the number of the proponent publication follows\nthe definition.\nThe proponent of ADP 6-0 is the United States Army Combined Arms Command. The preparing agency is\nthe Combined Arms Doctrine Directorate, Mission Command Center of Excellence. Send comments and\nrecommendations on a DA Form 2028 (Recommended Changes to Publications and Blank Forms) to\nCommander, U.S. Army Combined Arms Command and Fort Leavenworth, ATTN: ATZL-MCD (ADP 6-\n0), 300 McPherson Avenue, Fort Leavenworth, KS 66027-2337; by email to\nusarmy.leavenworth.mccoe.mbx.cadd-org-mailbox@army.mil; or submit an electronic DA Form 2028.\n[PDF p6]\nThis page intentionally left blank.\n[PDF p7]\n07 July 2026 ADP 6-0 v\nIntroduction\nCommand and control is fundamental to the art and science of warfare. It is the exercise of authority and\ndirection by a properly designated commander over assigned and attached forces. No single specialized\nmilitary function, either by itself or combined with others, has a purpose without it. Commanders are\nresponsible for command and control. Through command and control, commanders provide purpose and\ndirection to integrate all military activities towards a common goal—mission accomplishment.\nThe nature of war and the patterns of military history, however, show that war is not predictable or orderly.\nWar is both a contest of wills and a contest of intellect between two or more sides in conflict, with each trying\nto alter the behavior of the other side. The complexity of friendly and enemy organizations, unique\ncombinations of terrain and weather, and the dynamic interaction among all participants create uncertainty.\nChance and friction increase the potential for chaos during operations.\nAs the Army’s approach to command and control, mission command empowers subordinate decision making\nand decentralized execution appropriate to the situation. Mission command focuses on the objective of an\noperation, not on how to achieve it. It emphasizes timely decision making, understanding of the higher\ncommander’s intent, and the clear responsibility of subordinates to act within that intent to achieve the desired\nend state. With the commander’s intent to provide unity of effort, mission command relies on decentralized\nexecution and the initiative of subordinates.\nMission command requires tactically and technically competent commanders, staffs, and subordinate leaders\noperating in an environment of mutual trust and shared understanding. It requires building effective teams\nand a command climate in which commanders encourage subordinates to make decisions at the appropriate\nlevel and exercise disciplined initiative to seize opportunities and counter threats within the commander’s\nintent. Through mission orders, commanders focus their subordinates on the purpose of an operation rather\nthan on the details of how to perform assigned tasks. This allows subordinates the greatest possible freedom\nof action in the context of a particular situation. When delegating authority to subordinates, commanders set\nthe necessary conditions for success by allocating resources and describing levels of risk tolerance to\nsubordinates based on assigned tasks.\nAspects of mission command—including commander’s intent, mutual trust, disciplined initiative, and\nmission orders—have long been part of U.S. Army culture. General Grant’s orders to Sherman for the\ncampaign of 1864 and Sherman’s supporting plan are models of clear commander’s intent, mission orders,\nand understanding based on mutual trust (See vignette on page 11). A more recent example is the 3rd Infantry\nDivision’s march to Baghdad in 2003 and the subsequent “thunder runs” that showed the world that the Iraqi\nregime was defeated. Thunder Run was successful because the corps and division-level commanders\nestablished clear intent in their orders and trusted their subordinates’ judgment and abilities to exercise\ndisciplined initiative in response to a fluid, complex problem, underwriting the risks that they took.\nThe Army codified the term mission command and its principles in the 2003 edition of FM 6-0. As the\nArmy’s first manual dedicated to command and control, FM 6-0 addressed the fundamentals of command\nand control and described mission command as the Army’s preferred concept for exercising command and\ncontrol.\nThe 2012 edition of ADP 6-0 significantly revised Army command and control doctrine. It rescinded the\nterm command and control for Army forces and replaced it with mission command. The term mission\ncommand became an activity, a warfighting function, a system, and a philosophy. The use of the term mission\ncommand to describe multiple things created ambiguity. Mission command replaced command and control,\nbut in practical application it meant the same thing. This led to differing expectations among leadership\ncohorts regarding the appropriate application of mission command during operations and garrison activities.\nLabeling multiple things mission command eroded the importance of mission command as the approach to\nexercising command and control.\n[PDF p8]\nIntroduction\nvi ADP 6-0 07 July 2026\nThe 2019 edition of ADP 6-0 realigned Army command and control doctrine with joint, other service, and\nallied doctrine that use the term command and control. Like the 2003 edition of FM 6-0, the 2019 edition of\nADP 6-0 once again described the fundamentals of command and control and defined mission command as\nthe Army approach for its exercise.\nThis version of ADP 6-0 represents key updates to the 2019 edition as it relates to the current operating\nenvironment. Due to the vulnerabilities of large, static headquarters on the modern battlefield, command\nposts are no longer the singular center point of command and control. In order to remain survivable,\ncommanders organize and operate their command and control system in a dispersed manner through a series\nof command and control nodes. Other key updates and changes to this version of ADP 6-0 include—\n• Incorporating the Army’s operational concept of multidomain operations and related doctrine\nestablished in FM 3-0.\n• Accounting for technical advancements that enhance friendly command and control.\n• Accounting for threat capabilities that can affect friendly command and control.\n• Incorporating the role of technology and data in supporting the science of control and the command\nand control system.\n• Adjustments to the command and control system to include people, processes, data and networks,\nand command and control nodes.\nADP 6-0 consists of 4 chapters:\nChapter 1 sets the context for understanding command and control by describing the nature of war and\nsummarizing the Army’s operational concept. Next, it defines and describes command and control, their\nrelationship to each other, and their elements. The chapter then defines and describes mission command—\nthe Army’s approach to command and control. The chapter concludes with an overview of the command and\ncontrol warfighting function and the impact of technology on controlling operations.\nChapter 2 provides a discussion of the nature and elements of command. It then describes the role of the\ncommander in operations. The chapter concludes with a discussion on effective command.\nChapter 3 begins with a discussion of the nature of control and its elements. A discussion of control measures\nfollows. The chapter concludes with a discussion on effective control.\nChapter 4 defines and describes the command and control warfighting function. It begins with a discussion\nof the command and control tasks, followed by a discussion of the command and control system. The chapter\nconcludes with a discussion on organizing the command and control system.\nIntroductory Figure on page vii provides the ADP 6-0 logic map that shows how commanders provide\npurpose and direction to all operations through command and control, guided by the principles of mission\ncommand and executed through the command and control warfighting function.\nCertain terms for which ADP 6-0 is the proponent have been added or modified. The glossary contains\nacronyms and defined terms. See Introductory Table on page viii for a listing of new and modified Army\nterms."